module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "emitted_parse_refuses_a_grammatical_input_the_interpreter_accepts" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb },

  receipts: [
    "**THE EMITTED REALIZATION REFUSES, AT PARSE, A SOURCE THE GRAMMAR ACCEPTS AND THE INTERPRETER PARSES** (INVALID STATE: one source text is fed to TWO realizations of the SAME canonical .dag parse function -- parse_module_prepared_seeded -- and they give DIFFERENT verdicts. Under the interpreter (the native test route, native_test_context_from_ingest -> native_census_module_resolution) the text PARSES and refuses only later at LOWERING with else_less_if_unlowered. Under the emitted gen-1 binary (the native CLI acquisition route, acquisition_parsed -> parse_module_prepared -> parse_module_prepared_seeded) the SAME text refuses at PARSE with parse_g0_tokens_remain. The text is a module whose single fn body is an ELSE-LESS if followed by a tail statement: `fn f(c: Bool, i: Int) -> Int {` / `  if c { i }` / `  i` / `}` (the rtp_guard_fallthrough_source fixture, byte-identical across the three cc2-ceprobe runs, member file sha256 0f785394e803ce694370e7c917d7faad069b70bfabc2eb2167cd2160dcf409a8; emit.err sha256 837b2a71). The interpreter is RIGHT: the grammar production dag_grammar_if_block_form is `seq(fn_body, optional(if_else_tail))` (dag.dag:2414), so an else-less `if c { i }` is a valid block-form member and the trailing `i` is the next statement; the whole module parses and the else-less refusal belongs at lowering)",

    "**LOCALIZATION (underway; pinned to the earliest link by the emitted fragment, not asserted)** -- algebra first: the module production is a sequence(), which accepts only when EVERY element accepts, and parse_expr_repeat (parse.dag:4286) ALWAYS returns Accepted. So the only way a well-formed module reaches `accepted but remaining non-empty -> parse_g0_tokens_remain` is an emitted link that lets the module ACCEPT while leaving tokens unconsumed. RANKED CANDIDATE LINKS (the emitted v2_compiler_parse.rs fragment decides which): (i) the if_expr CHOICE alternative-selection -- a ChoiceByToken plan (parse_choice_plan / compile_choice_alts, parse.dag:3061) that COMMITS to one alternative on the next token without the interpreter's fallback, so on `if c { i }` it can fail the block-form path the interpreter reaches; (ii) the optional(...) ABSENT case (parse_expr_optional, parse.dag:4195) letting a rejected element's failure escape instead of converting it to Accepted-with-tokens-unchanged; (iii) parse_expr_repeat's stop/progress condition or parse_expr_terminal's token-class match. RULED OUT BY READING: the rc-guard path does NOT carry parse_expr_optional's OWN match -- both its arms (Accepted and Rejected) bind only plain fields, so analyze_rc_pattern (05_emit_rust.dag:11276) leaves needs_rc_pattern=false and emit_typed_match_arm (05_emit_rust.dag:15698) emits them as a correct no-guard discriminant match. The bug is therefore in the shared emitted realization of a grammar combinator (choice / optional / repeat / terminal), not in a single arm's pattern",

    "**WHAT MAKES IT RECURRING** (one shared emitted realization of the grammar's combinators -- not one grammar rule): the ~50 dag_grammar_optional sites, the choice sites (e.g. dag_grammar_if_expr's seq(expr, choice(if_then_form, if_block_form)) at dag.dag:2441), and the repeat/terminal matchers all reach ONE emitted realization built by the seed emitter (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) from the .dag grammar interpreter. A wrong decision about a combinator -- a first-token commit that should fall back, an absent case that should accept -- is one class across every site that uses that combinator, not one rule. A WHOLESALE break is self-excluded: the emitted compiler parses thousands of well-formed modules, so the fault is specific to the shape where a combinator's decision flips the verdict",

    "**WHY IT IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF COVERAGE** (the interpreter is RIGHT, so no interpreted claim reddens -- the same blind spot the freemonoid list_init miscompile had, whose fix comment records 'THE INTERPRETER LOWERS ALL OF THESE CORRECTLY, which is why no interpreted claim discriminates the class'). A text-match emitted witness (compile_dag_rust_emit_check) CANNOT distinguish the two verdicts, because BOTH the wrong and the right realization compile and emit; per the nested_pattern row's N7-1 rule a text check 'cannot tell the two apart, so a probe written with it would be a decoration.' The only witness that discriminates is a BUILT-RUN differential: the same source bytes run through the interpreter AND the freshly-emitted gen-1 binary, asserting one parse verdict",

    "**RUNG FOUND AT** rung 1 -- one observed divergence between the two realizations on one real grammar input, reproduced three times on srv2 (cc2-ceprobe / ceprobe2 / ceprobe3, at main 0dc96e1e, seed gunbc 9e5ee601, gen-1 CLI 3ffe505e), with the interpreted control green (namespace_xl0 return_tail_position) and the emitted side red. No interpreter claim discriminates it, and no per-input built-run differential exists in the durable set yet",

    "**TRIGGER** the both-executor discriminating probe: build the seed gunbc, emit the gen-1, build the emitted crate, and run BOTH executors on the verbatim else-less member, expecting the SAME verdict (parse-OK -> refuse at lowering with else_less_if_unlowered). Red while the emitted realization is wrong; green once it matches the interpreter. Durable interpreted side: ce_probe_elseless_text in v2.test.claim.self_host.closure_emission.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE** wherever a construct is realized independently by the interpreter and by the emission target, ask what fact decides its behaviour and where that fact is DECLARED. For the grammar's combinators the deciding facts are the parse-result discriminants and the token classes the interpreter reads; any emitted arm or plan that decides on anything OTHER than those -- a first-token commit where the interpreter falls back, a field compare, an arm-order tie-break, an absent case that propagates instead of accepting -- is a candidate for this class. Do NOT special-case one grammar rule (do not name if_else_tail): the class is the shared emitted realization of a grammar combinator diverging from the interpreted .dag on one input.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_that_falls_through_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_as_the_last_statement_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
